package com.hpcai270

// Days 6–10 — expanded field notes
val hpcDaysPart2 = listOf(
Day(6,"Parallel computing","One problem, many workers","Parallel computing means dividing a workload so multiple execution units can make progress at the same time. The goal is not simply to own more machines; the application must be able to use them efficiently.\n\nAnalogy: one person sorting 1,000 boxes takes a long time. Ten people can sort ten groups at once — if the work can be divided.","TYPES OF PARALLELISM\n\nData parallelism → many workers process different pieces of data.\nTask parallelism → different workers perform different tasks.\nNode-level parallelism → many cores/threads inside one server.\nCluster-level parallelism → many servers cooperate.\n\nThe more coordination required, the more the network and synchronization matter.","REAL HPC\nWeather models, molecular simulation, computational fluid dynamics and AI training all use parallelism. NVIDIA GPU clusters push the same principle much further: many GPU engines process tensors concurrently.","STORAGE CONNECTION\nA parallel application can request data from thousands of workers at once. If storage can serve only a small stream, the compute fleet waits. This is why HPC storage engineering is performance engineering, not just capacity management.","WHAT TO REMEMBER\nMore workers ≠ automatically more performance. Watch parallel efficiency, synchronization, communication overhead, storage throughput and application scaling.","Draw one workload split across 8 workers. Mark where synchronization and storage can become bottlenecks."),
Day(7,"MPI","Communication across nodes","MPI = Message Passing Interface. It is a standard programming model/API used by processes that may run on different machines. Each process normally has its own address space and explicitly exchanges messages.","MENTAL MODEL\n\nNode A: Process 0 ─────► Node B: Process 1\n              message\nNode B: Process 1 ─────► Node C: Process 2\n\nMPI applications often use collectives such as broadcast, reduce and all-to-all in addition to point-to-point communication.","WHY STORAGE PEOPLE SHOULD CARE\nMPI traffic is sensitive to network behavior. Bandwidth affects how much data moves per second; latency affects how quickly communication begins. At scale, small inefficiencies multiply across many processes.","REAL STACK\nLinux → NIC/HCA → network fabric → MPI library → application.\n\nCommon HPC implementations include Open MPI and MPICH. Slurm can allocate the nodes and launch distributed jobs.","AI CONNECTION\nDistributed AI frameworks use collective communication heavily. NVIDIA NCCL provides GPU-focused collectives; the same underlying principle applies: many workers exchange data to behave like one larger computation.","Know: rank, process, communicator, point-to-point communication and collective communication.","Explain why an MPI job can be affected by network latency even when CPU and storage are fast."),
Day(8,"OpenMP","Parallelism inside a node","OpenMP is a shared-memory programming model. A process can create multiple threads that execute portions of a workload concurrently while sharing the node's memory address space.","ONE NODE\n\nCore 1 ─┐\nCore 2 ─┤\nCore 3 ─┼── shared memory\nCore 4 ─┤\nCore 5 ─┘\n\nOpenMP commonly uses compiler directives such as #pragma omp parallel and #pragma omp for.","MPI vs OPENMP\n\nMPI → processes, commonly across nodes; explicit communication.\nOpenMP → threads, usually within one shared-memory node.\n\nReal applications can combine both: MPI across nodes + OpenMP inside each node.","PERFORMANCE TRAP\nAdding threads does not guarantee proportional speedup. Shared-memory contention, NUMA effects, synchronization and memory bandwidth can limit scaling.","WHY THIS MATTERS FOR AI/HPC\nThe application stack determines what hardware is actually exercised. A storage administrator moving into HPC needs enough compute knowledge to recognize whether an apparent storage problem is really an application or compute problem.","Know: process vs thread, shared memory, synchronization, NUMA and hybrid MPI+OpenMP.","Compare MPI and OpenMP in five sentences. Then draw a hybrid 2-node job."),
Day(9,"HPC storage","Throughput + concurrency","HPC storage is designed around a different question from a traditional file server. Instead of asking only 'How much capacity do we have?', ask: 'Can hundreds or thousands of clients read and write the required data concurrently without starving compute?'","THREE PERFORMANCE NUMBERS\n\nThroughput → GB/s or TB/s\nIOPS → operations per second\nLatency → time for an I/O operation\n\nWorkloads also have block size, read/write ratio, sequential/random behavior, queue depth and concurrency.","PARALLEL I/O\n\nClient 1 ─┐\nClient 2 ─┤\nClient 3 ─┼──► parallel filesystem ──► many storage targets\nClient N ─┘\n\nThe architecture spreads work rather than forcing every client through one disk or one server.","PRODUCTS TO RECOGNIZE\nDDN EXAScaler → Lustre-based HPC/AI filesystem.\nIBM Storage Scale → massively parallel file/data platform.\nDell PowerScale → scale-out NAS used in enterprise and AI/HPC scenarios.\nWEKA / VAST → modern high-performance data platforms.","THE GPU-STARVATION IDEA\nA GPU can calculate extremely quickly, but it still needs input data and must write checkpoints. If the data path cannot keep up, expensive compute resources spend time waiting.","NEXT SKILL\nLearn to measure instead of guessing. FIO, IOR, iostat, sar and application telemetry will become your instruments.","Why can a storage system that looks fast for one client perform badly with 1,000 concurrent clients?"),
Day(10,"Parallel filesystems","Lustre, GPFS and BeeGFS","A parallel filesystem is built so many clients can access file data concurrently across multiple servers and storage targets. This is a core HPC storage concept.","LUSTRE MENTAL MODEL\n\nClients → MDS/MDT → metadata\nClients → OSS/OST → file data\n\nMDS = Metadata Server\nMDT = Metadata Target\nOSS = Object Storage Server\nOST = Object Storage Target\n\nMetadata answers questions such as what a file is and where its metadata lives; data targets hold the file contents.","WHY SPLIT METADATA AND DATA?\nA directory operation and a multi-terabyte sequential read are different workloads. Separating their services allows the filesystem to scale and tune them independently.","OTHER NAMES\nIBM Storage Scale evolved from GPFS and is heavily used in HPC/AI. BeeGFS is another parallel filesystem used in technical computing. The implementations differ, but the central idea is parallel access to data.","STRIPING\nA large file can be distributed across multiple storage targets. Multiple clients can then participate in the I/O path. Striping decisions affect throughput, small-I/O behavior and contention.","FIELD RULE\nNever memorize acronyms without understanding the data path. In an interview, draw the path from client → metadata → data target and explain what happens during open, read and write.","Draw a Lustre architecture with 4 clients, 2 metadata services and 4 data targets. Explain where file metadata and file data go.")
)
